module std.source_annotation
// The language-agnostic authority for source annotations, per DESIGN 4c. It names no .dag syntax and no lexical rule: a realization decides what its annotation syntax looks like and routes it through its own annotation channel, then lands rows of THIS shape. Both the v1 seed tokenizer and the v2 modeled tokenizer import from here rather than each minting a placement enum, because two spellings of one concept is the nicknaming defect this whole lane exists to remove.




// Placement is OBSERVED at capture, not reconstructed later, because it cannot be reconstructed later. Whitespace and line structure are consumed before the parser sees anything, so once they are gone `fn a() -> Int { 1 } // text` followed by `fn b()` leaves residue identical to a leading annotation on b — two opposite meanings, one byte pattern. LeadingAfterLineIndent means only indentation preceded the annotation on its line; TrailingAfterSemanticToken means a semantic token did. Only the leading form is eligible for attachment in the first realization; trailing refuses until its attachment rule is separately modeled.
type AnnotationPlacement
  = LeadingAfterLineIndent
  | TrailingAfterSemanticToken


// What a tokenizer produces before any structural subject is known — hence UNBOUND. It is the shape that crosses the lexer-to-parser seam, and it deliberately carries no subject and no identity: binding is the parser's job, and minting an identity here would draw from the semantic occurrence allocator.

// WHY THIS LIVES IN std RATHER THAN IN A TOKENIZER: the v1 seed tokenizer cannot import v2 modules, so a capture shape declared inside either realization forces the other to mint a second spelling of one concept. v2's lexing module additionally carries an annotation_class Symbol on its own capture row because that lexer is generic over many languages with many annotation syntaxes; the v1 .dag tokenizer has exactly one, so it uses this shape directly. That is a real difference in what the two realizations must distinguish, not a fork — and both converge on SourceAnnotationDebt once a subject is known, which is the row that actually persists.

// THE FIELD IS `lexeme`, NOT `text`, AND THE DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING. A capture carries RAW SYNTAX exactly as it appeared, delimiter included: the lexer reports what it saw, and the language-specific attachment realization owns normalization into SourceAnnotationDebt.text. Naming it `text` while storing a `//`-prefixed lexeme would make the two indistinguishable at the type level, and the failure is not hypothetical — if one realization strips the delimiter and another does not, parse then render then parse either doubles the delimiter or silently changes annotation content, and the D-C round-trip control is what would catch it. Every realization capturing into this field stores raw; every realization reading it for durable text strips.
// TWO ADJACENCY BITS, BOTH OBSERVED AT CAPTURE, BECAUSE NEITHER IS RECOVERABLE LATER. `preceded_by_blank_line` says the previous line is whitespace only — it decides where one authored block ENDS. `preceded_by_annotation_line` says the previous line is itself a standalone annotation line — it decides that this capture CONTINUES the block that line opened, which is what makes a block's subject a fact about its first line rather than something re-derived per line. The pair is not redundant: a capture with both false sits directly under a code line, and that is the only shape eligible to open a module-header block.
type UnboundAnnotationCapture {
  lexeme: String
  origin: std.types.SourceSpan
  placement: AnnotationPlacement
  preceded_by_blank_line: Bool
  preceded_by_annotation_line: Bool
}


// Every inhabitant is MODELING DEBT — that is the type's standing law, not a field on it. A comment is prose that the executable model does not currently represent, retained honestly rather than deleted or promoted. There is deliberately no rationale variant: a permanent category with no authoring path, no distinct consumer, and no lifecycle would be speculative vocabulary, and offered early every unmodeled invariant acquires a why-prefix and declares itself permanent. A second population earns its own carrier when migration produces one and its consumer is modeled.

// NOTE WHAT IS ABSENT: this row has no identity of its own. Giving an annotation an OccurrenceId would draw from the graph-scoped semantic occurrence allocator, so inserting a comment could shift the identities of declarations later in the same source and in later-parsed modules — the exact hazard DESIGN 4c forbids. `subject` REFERS to a semantic occurrence; it never mints one. Multiplicity and source order are carried by the ordered graph below, not by per-row ids, and a dedicated SourceAnnotationId arrives only when a real consumer must address one annotation independently.
type SourceAnnotationDebt {
  subject: std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId
  text: String
  origin: std.types.SourceSpan
}


// An ORDERED sequence, because order and multiplicity are the whole of an annotation's identity here. Two annotations with the same text on the same subject are two annotations, and the one written first is written first — a set would silently merge them and a map would need the per-row key this carrier deliberately does not have. Consecutive leading lines group into one block at attachment, so a block is one row whose text carries its own line breaks, not N rows a consumer must re-associate.
type SourceAnnotationGraph {
  rows: List<SourceAnnotationDebt>
}

fn source_annotation_graph_empty() -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  SourceAnnotationGraph { rows: [] }
}

fn source_annotation_graph_rows(graph: SourceAnnotationGraph) -> List<SourceAnnotationDebt> {
  graph.rows
}


// Joins the graphs of separately parsed sources, preserving order. It exists so a consumer accumulating across a compilation never reaches into `rows` to append — the ordered-sequence law lives with the carrier, and a caller that rebuilt the record itself would be free to sort, dedupe, or reverse it and silently lose the multiplicity the order encodes.
fn source_annotation_graph_concat(
  left: SourceAnnotationGraph,
  right: SourceAnnotationGraph
) -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  SourceAnnotationGraph { rows: concat(left.rows, right.rows) }
}


// Refusals are typed and located rather than dropped, because a dropped annotation is indistinguishable from a source that never carried one — the empty-observation narrow DESIGN names. UnattachedAtScopeEnd is the pending-block-at-EOF case: prose that names no subject. TrailingNotModeled and BodyGrainNotModeled are the two deliberate first-cut narrowings; each names what would have to be modeled to admit it, so neither reads as an oversight.
type AnnotationAttachmentRefusal
  = UnattachedAtScopeEnd { origin: std.types.SourceSpan }
  | TrailingNotModeled { origin: std.types.SourceSpan }
  | BodyGrainNotModeled { origin: std.types.SourceSpan }


// Every refusal carries where it happened, so a diagnostic reporting one does not need to carry a second copy of the span beside it. A consumer asks this rather than storing the origin again — a duplicated span is a second representation that can disagree with the first.
fn annotation_attachment_refusal_origin(refusal: AnnotationAttachmentRefusal) -> std.types.SourceSpan {
  match refusal {
    UnattachedAtScopeEnd { origin: origin } => origin
    TrailingNotModeled { origin: origin } => origin
    BodyGrainNotModeled { origin: origin } => origin
  }
}


// The human-facing sentence for each refusal, single-authored here beside the variants so a new refusal cannot be added without one. Each says what was seen and what would have to be modeled to admit it, because the reader's next question after `this is refused` is always `then where do I put it`.
fn annotation_attachment_refusal_message(refusal: AnnotationAttachmentRefusal) -> String {
  match refusal {
    UnattachedAtScopeEnd { origin: _ } =>
      "source annotation names no subject: no module item follows it. Move it above the declaration it describes."
    TrailingNotModeled { origin: _ } =>
      "source annotation follows code on its own line. Trailing placement has no attachment rule yet; move it to its own line above the declaration."
    BodyGrainNotModeled { origin: _ } =>
      "source annotation sits inside a declaration body. Only module-item grain is modeled; move it above the declaration it describes."
  }
}

type AnnotationAttachment
  = AnnotationAttached { row: SourceAnnotationDebt }
  | AnnotationRefused { refusal: AnnotationAttachmentRefusal }


type NonEmptyAnnotationAttachmentRefusals {
  head: AnnotationAttachmentRefusal
  tail: List<AnnotationAttachmentRefusal>
}

type AnnotationAdmission
  = AnnotationsAdmitted { graph: SourceAnnotationGraph }
  | AnnotationsRefused { refusals: NonEmptyAnnotationAttachmentRefusals }

fn non_empty_refusals_all(
  refusals: NonEmptyAnnotationAttachmentRefusals
) -> List<AnnotationAttachmentRefusal> {
  concat([refusals.head], refusals.tail)
}

// The gate between analysing attachment and CONTINUING with it. AnnotationAttachmentResult is an analysis surface — it reports what attached and what did not, and a reader may legitimately inspect both. But a caller that takes `graph` from that record and carries on has done exactly what admitting `//` and discarding the capture would have done: the prose is dropped and compilation proceeds as though the source never carried it.
data annotation_admission_note: String = "WHAT THIS ACTUALLY GUARANTEES, AT ITS HONEST RUNG. Both production v1 frontend paths route through this function, and on that path a refusal withholds the graph entirely and blocks emitted output. That is a ROUTING INVARIANT enforced by every caller using one seam — it is NOT a construction impossibility. `AnnotationAttachmentResult` is a plain record and `annotation_attachment_result_graph` returns its graph without consulting admission, so a newly written caller could bypass this and nothing would stop it.\n\nThe two fail differently, and the distinction is why this paragraph exists rather than a stronger-sounding one. A routing invariant holds while every caller goes through the seam and breaks SILENTLY the first time one does not; an impossibility cannot break, because the bypass has no representation. Calling the first the second is the rung inflation DESIGN 4b names as worse than sitting low, since an inflated class never ranks for climbing.\n\nNEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, and it is small and known: make the bypass unrepresentable — remove the unadmitted graph accessor and seal the result carrier so a graph can only be obtained through admission — landed with a discriminating control proving a bypass does not compile. Until that exists, this is a mechanically preventive wall on the production path, not a language-level guarantee.\n\nThe refusal payload is a NON-EMPTY carrier, which does make `refused, with nothing to report` unwritable rather than merely unlikely — a refusal arm holding an empty list would be a silent success wearing a refusal's name.\n\nThis is deliberately all-or-nothing per compilation unit. A partial admission — take the rows that bound, ignore the ones that did not — is the absorbing fallback in its authored form: the deficit's frequency drops to zero, nothing counts it, and the unattachable prose quietly stops existing."
fn admit_annotations(result: AnnotationAttachmentResult) -> AnnotationAdmission {
  match first(result.refusals) {
    Absent => AnnotationsAdmitted { graph: result.graph }
    Present { value: head } =>
      AnnotationsRefused {
        refusals: NonEmptyAnnotationAttachmentRefusals {
          head: head,
          tail: skip(result.refusals, 1),
        },
      }
  }
}


// The first realization admits leading annotations only, and this is the single predicate that says so. A realization asking whether to attach consults this rather than re-deciding, so widening the admitted grain later is one edit here plus the attachment rule it enables, never a sweep over every call site.
fn annotation_placement_is_attachable(placement: AnnotationPlacement) -> Bool {
  match placement {
    LeadingAfterLineIndent => true
    TrailingAfterSemanticToken => false
  }
}


fn code_point_is_line_feed(cp: Int) -> Bool {
  cp == 10
}

fn code_point_is_indent(cp: Int) -> Bool {
  cp == 32 || cp == 9
}


// Placement is decided by the PHYSICAL LINE PREFIX — the raw text between the last line feed and the annotation — and never by which lexical category the preceding lexeme belonged to.

// The category guess is wrong, and a real grammar disproves it. Deciding `leading` by `no semantic token seen on this line yet` assumes line boundaries live in trivia; the Python model's own token lexemes include a closing-paren-plus-newline-plus-indent sequence, which ENDS on a fresh line carrying only indentation, and a newline-plus-indent-plus-if sequence, which ends on a fresh line carrying semantic text. An annotation after the first is leading and after the second is trailing, yet a category rule classifies both as trailing because in both cases the last thing consumed was a semantic token. Any grammar whose tokens may span lines breaks the same way.

// So the state is `line prefix is indent only`, advanced by the RAW TEXT of every consumed lexeme regardless of category. One fold expresses the whole rule: a line feed resets the prefix to empty (vacuously indent-only), and every character after it must be indentation for the prefix to stay so. A lexeme with no line feed therefore preserves the previous state only while it is itself indentation, and a lexeme containing one is judged entirely by what follows its LAST line feed — both cases the fold produces without a special arm. An empty lexeme preserves the state.

// A lexer that walks lexemes threads this forward; a lexer with random access to the source feeds it the raw slice it consumed between steps. Both consume THIS function, because two spellings of one placement rule would let the realizations disagree about the same source.
fn advance_line_prefix_indent_only(previous: Bool, code_points: List<Int>) -> Bool {
  fold(
    code_points,
    init: previous,
    f: (acc, cp) =>
      if code_point_is_line_feed(cp: cp) { true } else { acc && code_point_is_indent(cp: cp) }
  )
}

// The rule is stated over CODE POINTS, not over a String, because the two realizations do not share a string representation: the seed's String is a host string while the modeled compiler's is already a sequence of code points. Taking the sequence as the parameter puts the one representation-specific step — how to get code points out of whatever this realization calls a lexeme — at each call site, and keeps the rule itself single-authored. The text-shaped convenience below exists for host-string callers and is the same fold, never a second rule.
fn advance_line_prefix_indent_only_text(previous: Bool, lexeme: String) -> Bool {
  advance_line_prefix_indent_only(previous: previous, code_points: chars(s: lexeme))
}

fn placement_from_line_prefix(indent_only: Bool) -> AnnotationPlacement {
  if indent_only { LeadingAfterLineIndent } else { TrailingAfterSemanticToken }
}


// What a realization offers as attachable subjects, and deliberately NOT DeclarationOccurrence. Two reasons, both structural. First, this module would otherwise depend on the whole occurrence transport to express a rule that needs exactly two facts per subject — an identity to refer to and a span to compare against. Second, the roster IS where the module-item narrowing lives: a realization admitting only module-scope declarations supplies only those, and widening to a finer grain later is a change in what the caller enumerates, not a change to the fold. The occurrence is REFERRED to, never minted; every id in a roster was already allocated by the semantic parser before attachment runs.
// `module_root` says this subject IS the module declaration itself, stated by the realization because only the realization knows — the fold sees identities and spans, and guessing the module structurally (say, as the earliest subject by start) would misread any roster whose earliest subject is an ordinary member. The module-header rule below fires only for a roster that declares one.
type AnnotationSubject {
  occurrence: std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId
  span: std.types.SourceSpan
  module_root: Bool
}


// The capture after the realization has stripped its delimiter and before a subject is known. It exists as a distinct type from UnboundAnnotationCapture precisely because the `lexeme` versus `text` distinction is load-bearing: normalization is language-specific and happens exactly once, at the boundary between the realization's capture and this shared fold, so the fold can never be handed raw syntax by accident. A realization that forgot to strip would have to construct this type to call at all, and the field name is what tells it.
type NormalizedAnnotationCapture {
  text: String
  origin: std.types.SourceSpan
  placement: AnnotationPlacement
  preceded_by_blank_line: Bool
  preceded_by_annotation_line: Bool
}


// Both halves of the outcome, because either alone is a lie. The graph alone hides that prose was dropped; the refusals alone hide what was kept. A consumer reading only `graph` still gets a correct graph — it simply is not entitled to conclude the source carried nothing else, and the refusal list is what makes that conclusion checkable rather than assumed.
type AnnotationAttachmentResult {
  graph: SourceAnnotationGraph
  refusals: List<AnnotationAttachmentRefusal>
}

fn annotation_attachment_result_graph(result: AnnotationAttachmentResult) -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  result.graph
}

fn annotation_attachment_result_refusals(result: AnnotationAttachmentResult) -> List<AnnotationAttachmentRefusal> {
  result.refusals
}


type AnnotationSubjectPick {
  contained: Bool
  following: AnnotationSubject?
}

// Resolves one capture against the subject roster in a single pass, answering both questions the attachment rule asks: is the capture INSIDE some subject (body grain, refused), and what is the nearest subject STARTING AFTER it (its leading subject). Containment is checked before proximity because the two overlap — prose inside a function body always has some later declaration after it, and picking that one would silently reattach body commentary to the next unrelated declaration. That is the specific misattachment BodyGrainNotModeled exists to prevent, and checking proximity first would produce a plausible wrong answer rather than a refusal.

// POST-MODULE HEADER GAP: prose on the line immediately after `module p.q` attaches to the module, not the first depth-1 member. A blank line between the module declaration and the comment starts a new block that follows the ordinary nearest-following rule instead. Without this arm, a line-2 comment would skip the module (its span ends before the capture) and bind to the first import or declaration — the wrong subject for module-level rationale while still satisfying line-1 CI layout.

// Adjacency to the module line needs no width constant. The two observed bits pin the shape exactly: a capture that is neither blank-preceded nor annotation-preceded has NON-COMMENT CONTENT on the line above it, and if it also starts after the module subject and ends before the first member starts, the only thing that line can be is the module declaration itself — a member there would put the capture past `first_member_start`. A capture that IS annotation-preceded is a continuation line; its block's subject was decided by the block's first line, so the arm refuses it and the attach fold merges it instead. An earlier revision used a tolerance window (`span.end + 12`) in place of the annotation-line bit; that both under-covered long dotted module names (the window is a guess about how many characters a module declaration spans) and mis-fired on the adjacent second line of a blank-separated block below the module, which the window happened to exclude only when the module name was short.

// The roster is scanned rather than indexed, so resolution is captures times subjects. That is a join without a sorted index, not a copied accumulator, and it is bounded by one source's declarations.
data annotation_subject_pick_note: String = "DISSOLVE-ON: a span-ordered subject index exists on the parse artifact; then the nearest-following pick becomes a lookup and this fold reads it."

fn module_root_pick_step(acc: AnnotationSubject?, row: AnnotationSubject) -> AnnotationSubject? {
  if row.module_root {
    match acc {
      Absent => Present { value: row }
      Present { value: best } =>
        if row.span.start < best.span.start { Present { value: row } } else { acc }
    }
  } else {
    acc
  }
}

fn module_root_subject(subjects: List<AnnotationSubject>) -> AnnotationSubject? {
  fold(
    subjects,
    init: none,
    f: (acc, row) => module_root_pick_step(acc: acc, row: row)
  )
}

fn earliest_member_start_after(subjects: List<AnnotationSubject>, after: Int, fallback: Int) -> Int {
  fold(
    subjects,
    init: fallback,
    f: (best, row) =>
      if row.span.start > after && row.span.start < best {
        row.span.start
      } else {
        best
      }
  )
}

fn module_header_gap_subject(
  subjects: List<AnnotationSubject>,
  origin: std.types.SourceSpan,
  preceded_by_blank_line: Bool,
  preceded_by_annotation_line: Bool
) -> AnnotationSubject? {
  if preceded_by_blank_line || preceded_by_annotation_line {
    none
  } else {
    match module_root_subject(subjects: subjects) {
      Absent => none
      Present { value: mod_subject } =>
        let first_member_start = earliest_member_start_after(
          subjects: subjects,
          after: mod_subject.span.end,
          fallback: origin.end + 1
        )
        if origin.start >= mod_subject.span.end
          && origin.end <= first_member_start {
          Present { value: mod_subject }
        } else {
          none
        }
    }
  }
}

fn annotation_subject_pick(
  subjects: List<AnnotationSubject>,
  origin: std.types.SourceSpan,
  preceded_by_blank_line: Bool,
  preceded_by_annotation_line: Bool
) -> AnnotationSubjectPick {
  match module_header_gap_subject(
    subjects: subjects,
    origin: origin,
    preceded_by_blank_line: preceded_by_blank_line,
    preceded_by_annotation_line: preceded_by_annotation_line
  ) {
    Present { value: mod_subject } =>
      AnnotationSubjectPick { contained: false, following: Present { value: mod_subject } }
    Absent =>
      fold(
        subjects,
        init: AnnotationSubjectPick { contained: false, following: none },
        f: (acc, subject) =>
          if origin.start >= subject.span.start && origin.end <= subject.span.end {
            AnnotationSubjectPick { contained: true, following: acc.following }
          } else if subject.span.start < origin.end {
            acc
          } else {
            match acc.following {
              Absent =>
                AnnotationSubjectPick { contained: acc.contained, following: Present { value: subject } }
              Present { value: best } =>
                if subject.span.start < best.span.start {
                  AnnotationSubjectPick { contained: acc.contained, following: Present { value: subject } }
                } else {
                  acc
                }
            }
          }
      )
  }
}





// WHY AGREEMENT IS KEYED ON A STRING AND NOT ON OccurrenceId. An occurrence identity is unique within ONE graph-scoped allocator. Two separately parsed sources have two allocators, so equal id VALUES across them establish equal allocation ordinals and nothing else — a round trip comparing them directly would report `same subject` for two declarations that merely occupy the same position in their respective files.

// That is not a hypothetical. Identical prose above `data alpha` in one source and above `data beta` in another source of the same shape yields the same ordinal for both, so an id-valued comparison agrees while the annotations name different declarations. The failure is a FALSE POSITIVE in the oracle, which is the direction that never gets noticed.

// So agreement is stated over a caller-supplied key that is stable ACROSS parses. The realization decides what that key is, because only the realization knows: for the first .dag cut it is the authored declaration name, which is unique among one module's items and is exactly what a reader means by `the same subject`. A realization whose subjects are not uniquely named must supply a qualified key instead.
data annotation_subject_key_note: String = "DISSOLVE-ON: when the containment tree is the single naming authority, the key becomes the containment path and uniqueness stops being a per-realization precondition."
type KeyedAnnotationRow {
  subject_key: String
  text: String
}


type KeyedAnnotationRowWalk {
  rest: List<KeyedAnnotationRow>
  agreed: Bool
}

// WHAT AGREEMENT MEANS, and deliberately what it does not. Parse a source, render its graph, parse the result: the two graphs agree when they have the same rows in the same order, each row naming the same subject and carrying the same normalized text.

// EXACT SOURCE BYTES ARE NOT THE TARGET, and cannot be. Normalization strips the delimiter and at most one following space, so `//tight` and `// tight` both land as `tight` and the authored difference is gone before any renderer runs. A property demanding original bytes would be unsatisfiable against this carrier — not hard, unsatisfiable — so it would either never pass or be quietly weakened until it passed. The declared canonical form replaces it: the renderer emits ONE spelling, and the property asserts the graph survives, not the keystrokes.

// FOUR THINGS ARE THEREFORE EXCLUDED ON PURPOSE. Original delimiter spacing, because normalization already discarded it. Original byte offsets, because rendered source has its own. Raw node equality, because v1 nodes carry spans and a span-carrying comparison reports PROVENANCE differences as semantic ones. And origin spans on the rows themselves — this predicate compares subject and text only, for the same reason: a rendered source legitimately places the same annotation at a different offset, and a comparison that failed on that would be measuring the renderer's line arithmetic rather than whether the prose survived.

// ORDER AND MULTIPLICITY ARE IN, not incidentally but because the carrier says they are its identity: two rows with the same text on the same subject are two annotations, and the first-written is first.
// Walks both row lists together rather than comparing counts and then contents. A count check followed by a per-row check reads as equivalent and is not — it answers `same size` and `these rows match somewhere` as two separate questions, and neither pins ORDER. Consuming both lists in step makes position part of the comparison by construction.

// It is a FOLD carrying the unconsumed right-hand rows, not a recursion over both. Graphs concatenate across compilation units, so row count scales with the corpus's whole prose population and a recursive comparison would make stack depth a function of how much prose the repository holds. The positional guarantee is identical either way; only the depth dependency differs.

// Length mismatch falls out of the walk rather than being checked separately: a right side that runs dry marks disagreement, and a right side with rows left over fails the final emptiness check.
fn keyed_annotation_rows_agree(
  left: List<KeyedAnnotationRow>,
  right: List<KeyedAnnotationRow>
) -> Bool {
  let walk = fold(
    left,
    init: KeyedAnnotationRowWalk { rest: right, agreed: true },
    f: (acc, row) =>
      match first(acc.rest) {
        Absent => KeyedAnnotationRowWalk { rest: [], agreed: false }
        Present { value: other } =>
          KeyedAnnotationRowWalk {
            rest: skip(acc.rest, 1),
            agreed: acc.agreed
              && row.subject_key == other.subject_key
              && row.text == other.text,
          }
      }
  )
  walk.agreed && (walk.rest |> count) == 0
}

// `pending_adjacent` records whether the immediately previous capture opened or extended `pending`. A continuation line (annotation-preceded, not blank-preceded) merges into `pending` only when this is true: captures arrive in source order, so the standalone comment line above a continuation IS the previous capture, and if that capture was refused rather than admitted, the continuation must not ride into whatever OLDER block still sits open — it falls through to the ordinary rule and is judged on its own.
type AnnotationAttachAcc {
  rows: List<SourceAnnotationDebt>
  refusals: List<AnnotationAttachmentRefusal>
  pending: SourceAnnotationDebt?
  pending_adjacent: Bool
}

fn annotation_attach_flush(acc: AnnotationAttachAcc) -> List<SourceAnnotationDebt> {
  match acc.pending {
    Absent => acc.rows
    Present { value: row } => list_push(acc.rows, row)
  }
}

// A BLANK LINE SPLITS A BLOCK. Two facts decide grouping — same following subject, and physical adjacency — and both are required.

// An earlier revision used same-subject alone and claimed those captures were `by construction consecutive`. They are not. Two comment lines directly above a declaration and two comment BLOCKS separated by a blank line above the SAME declaration both resolve to that one subject, so same-subject alone merged them into a single row joined by a single newline and the blank line ceased to exist. That is not a formatting nicety: the authored structure is gone, and once a renderer exists it would render one block where the author wrote two, permanently.

// Adjacency cannot be recovered here — the fold sees spans, not source, and the whitespace between two captures was consumed by the lexer — so it is OBSERVED at capture, exactly like placement and for exactly the same reason. `preceded_by_blank_line` is that observation, and this fold reads it rather than re-deriving it.

// The merged row's text carries its own line breaks and its origin spans first capture start to last capture end, so a block is addressable as one authored unit. `pending` holds the block still being extended; it is flushed when a capture resolves to a different subject, when a capture opens a new block after a blank line, and once more at the end.
// THE RENDERER'S OBLIGATION, stated here because it is the exact inverse of the grouping rule above and the two must not drift apart. A realization rendering a graph back to source MUST separate two consecutive rows sharing a subject by a BLANK LINE, and MUST render one row's embedded line breaks as adjacent annotation lines with no blank line between them.

// That is not a formatting preference — it is what makes the round trip mean anything. The grouping fold reads a blank line as `these are two authored blocks` and adjacency as `this is one block spanning two lines`. A renderer that emitted the rows adjacently would produce source that re-parses as ONE row where the author wrote two, and the round trip would then certify the merge the grouping rule exists to prevent. The failure is silent and it is permanent, because the merged form is what gets written back.

// What this law does NOT fix is the delimiter: how an annotation line is spelled is a realization fact, so each realization owns the inverse of its own normalization step and states it beside that step.
fn annotation_attach_step(
  acc: AnnotationAttachAcc,
  capture: NormalizedAnnotationCapture,
  subjects: List<AnnotationSubject>
) -> AnnotationAttachAcc {
  if !annotation_placement_is_attachable(placement: capture.placement) {
    AnnotationAttachAcc {
      rows: acc.rows,
      refusals: list_push(acc.refusals, TrailingNotModeled { origin: capture.origin }),
      pending: acc.pending,
      pending_adjacent: false,
    }
  } else {
    let pick = annotation_subject_pick(
      subjects: subjects,
      origin: capture.origin,
      preceded_by_blank_line: capture.preceded_by_blank_line,
      preceded_by_annotation_line: capture.preceded_by_annotation_line
    )
    if pick.contained {
      AnnotationAttachAcc {
        rows: acc.rows,
        refusals: list_push(acc.refusals, BodyGrainNotModeled { origin: capture.origin }),
        pending: acc.pending,
        pending_adjacent: false,
      }
    } else if capture.preceded_by_annotation_line && !capture.preceded_by_blank_line && acc.pending_adjacent {
      match acc.pending {
        Present { value: open } =>
          AnnotationAttachAcc {
            rows: acc.rows,
            refusals: acc.refusals,
            pending: Present {
              value: SourceAnnotationDebt {
                subject: open.subject,
                text: concat(open.text, concat("\n", capture.text)),
                origin: std.types.SourceSpan {
                  file: open.origin.file,
                  start: open.origin.start,
                  end: capture.origin.end,
                },
              },
            },
            pending_adjacent: true,
          }
        Absent =>
          annotation_attach_resolve(acc: acc, capture: capture, pick: pick)
      }
    } else {
      annotation_attach_resolve(acc: acc, capture: capture, pick: pick)
    }
  }
}

// A standalone leading capture with empty normalized text and no following subject is a lexical separator (bare // between module-scope declarations), not prose debt — skipping it is intentional, not UnattachedAtScopeEnd. That refusal applies only when capture.text is non-empty yet names no subject. Witness: source_annotation_attachment_witness_test w_empty_unattached_capture_skips_without_refusal vs w_nonempty_unattached_capture_still_refuses; v1_annotation_round_trip_test w_an_empty_annotation_line_survives for empty text ON a subject (admitted row).
// The ordinary (non-continuation) resolution: the capture's own pick decides its subject. A continuation line never reaches this — its subject is its block's first line's subject, held in `pending` — except when the line it continues was refused, in which case it is judged here on its own and typically shares that refusal.
fn annotation_attach_resolve(
  acc: AnnotationAttachAcc,
  capture: NormalizedAnnotationCapture,
  pick: AnnotationSubjectPick
) -> AnnotationAttachAcc {
  match pick.following {
    Absent =>
      if capture.text == "" {
        acc
      } else {
        AnnotationAttachAcc {
          rows: acc.rows,
          refusals: list_push(acc.refusals, UnattachedAtScopeEnd { origin: capture.origin }),
          pending: acc.pending,
          pending_adjacent: false,
        }
      }
    Present { value: subject } =>
      match acc.pending {
        Present { value: open } =>
          if open.subject.value == subject.occurrence.value && !capture.preceded_by_blank_line {
            AnnotationAttachAcc {
              rows: acc.rows,
              refusals: acc.refusals,
              pending: Present {
                value: SourceAnnotationDebt {
                  subject: open.subject,
                  text: concat(open.text, concat("\n", capture.text)),
                  origin: std.types.SourceSpan {
                    file: open.origin.file,
                    start: open.origin.start,
                    end: capture.origin.end,
                  },
                },
              },
              pending_adjacent: true,
            }
          } else {
            AnnotationAttachAcc {
              rows: list_push(acc.rows, open),
              refusals: acc.refusals,
              pending: Present {
                value: SourceAnnotationDebt {
                  subject: subject.occurrence,
                  text: capture.text,
                  origin: capture.origin,
                },
              },
              pending_adjacent: true,
            }
          }
        Absent =>
          AnnotationAttachAcc {
            rows: acc.rows,
            refusals: acc.refusals,
            pending: Present {
              value: SourceAnnotationDebt {
                subject: subject.occurrence,
                text: capture.text,
                origin: capture.origin,
              },
            },
            pending_adjacent: true,
          }
      }
  }
}


// The single attachment authority both realizations call. It is a PURE FOLD over captures and an already-allocated subject roster: it reads occurrence identities and never mints one, so inserting, editing, or deleting a comment cannot shift any declaration's identity here or in any later-parsed module. That property is not a convention this function happens to honor — there is no allocator in scope for it to violate.

// Captures must arrive in source order, which is the order the lexer produced them; the block grouping above is the only thing that depends on it.
fn attach_annotations(
  captures: List<NormalizedAnnotationCapture>,
  subjects: List<AnnotationSubject>
) -> AnnotationAttachmentResult {
  let acc = fold(
    captures,
    init: AnnotationAttachAcc { rows: [], refusals: [], pending: none, pending_adjacent: false },
    f: (acc, capture) =>
      annotation_attach_step(acc: acc, capture: capture, subjects: subjects)
  )
  AnnotationAttachmentResult {
    graph: SourceAnnotationGraph { rows: annotation_attach_flush(acc: acc) },
    refusals: acc.refusals,
  }
}
